# Repository Agent Rules: <PROJECT_NAME>

> STARTER TEMPLATE — copy to the target repository as `AGENTS.md`.
> Keep this `.example` file inert in shared/global configuration directories.
> Fill every `<PLACEHOLDER>`, choose explicit policies, and delete inapplicable
> sections and these setup notes. Do not run placeholder commands.

## Starter checklist

1. Fill PROJECT.md first: product scope, stack, architecture, contracts, and
   acceptance requirements. Reference it here rather than duplicating it.
   Draft work from BACKLOG.md.example using stable IDs, dependencies, and acceptance
   criteria. Human/chat/generated drafts stay `[-]` until reviewed and made ready.
2. Complete the source-of-truth table and setup/check commands. Verify commands
   from their specified directories; identify prerequisites for unavailable checks.
3. Define local conventions, generated/protected paths, and dependency rules.
   Fill the security model in PROJECT.md and select actual security checks here.
4. Choose interactive/automation policies, review requirements, commit
   authorization, and one owner for task state and integration.
5. If using a backlog/runner, create its inputs and verify its actual state
   format, installed agent names, working directory, recovery, and runtime limits.
   This document does not implement or guarantee those runner behaviors.
   Select an installed workflow skill or describe repository-specific Git rules
   here. Shared workflow details belong in the skill; product requirements belong
   in PROJECT.md.
6. For UI work, identify design sources and how to obtain rendered evidence.
   For multi-repo work, identify contract owners and integration responsibilities.
 7. Confirm `tech-lead`, `architect`, `developer`, `senior-developer`, `ui-designer`, `reviewer`, and `docs` are available
   where needed. As the project owner, install and verify any required skills/tools
   in this repository before automation. Agents discover existing installations;
   they must not initialize or update skills to resolve missing dependencies.
8. Complete one small end-to-end task to check instructions, permissions, checks,
   handoffs, and reporting before starting unattended work.

## Sources of truth and scope

| Concern | Canonical location | Instructions |
|---|---|---|
| Product, stack, architecture, acceptance | `PROJECT.md` | Read relevant sections; do not invent missing decisions. |
| Planned work | `<BACKLOG_FILE_OR_ISSUE_TRACKER_OR_NONE>` | Scope comes from the current request or explicit assignment. |
| Shared system contracts | `<UNIFIED_SPEC_PATH_OR_NONE>` | Cross-repo contracts take precedence over local guesses. |
| Schemas and architecture decisions | `<CANONICAL_PATHS_OR_NONE>` | Link definitions rather than making competing copies. |
| Design system and overrides | `<ACTUAL_PATHS_OR_NONE>` | Read for relevant UI work; paths may be nested by application. |
| Generated artifacts | `<PATHS_AND_SOURCE_LOCATIONS_OR_NONE>` | Follow the generation policy; do not hand-edit generated output. |

Follow applicable instruction hierarchy and repository-local rules. The explicit
user request/assignment defines task scope; a backlog does not prohibit interactive
questions, reviews, or maintenance requests. Surface contradictions with canonical
contracts before changing the affected behavior. Continue independent, well-defined
work where possible.

## Start of work

### Main agent / coordinator

1. Identify request mode and acceptance criteria. Read relevant PROJECT.md
   sections, local instructions, and shared contracts. Avoid rereading unchanged
   context or unrelated specifications on every handoff.
2. Check the repository/worktree and initial changes. Use `git status --short`
   and relevant recent history when Git is present. Record the task baseline
   commit and existing edits before new work. Do not initialize a repository,
   discard changes, or create commits implicitly.
3. Locate actual check commands and dependencies. Report missing prerequisites;
   do not present environmental failures as implementation defects.
4. Decide whether architect, developer, ui-designer, reviewer, or docs adds value.
   Keep small tasks direct; retain explicit planning for complex work.

### Assigned subagents

Read the assignment and relevant project context. Stay within assigned scope;
do not select other backlog items, change completion status, or commit. Use supplied
baseline/results/artifact paths rather than repeating the entire startup procedure.
Return missing context and issues to the coordinator. Do not recursively delegate
unless explicitly configured and assigned.

### Runner-launched work — remove if unused

- Runner entry point: `<RUNNER_PATH_AND_INVOCATION>`.
- Assigned-work identification: `<EXACT_ITEM_IDS_OR_STATE_MARKERS_AND_BATCH_FORMAT>`.
- Time/attempt limits and recovery: `<ACTUAL_RUNNER_BEHAVIOR_AND_LIMITS>`.
- Execution root and branch/base policy: `<PROJECT_SANDBOX_CHECKOUT_AND_TASK_BRANCH_POLICY>`.
- Exclusive ownership/locking: `<RUNNER_OR_OWNER_MECHANISM>`.
- Install `scripts/backlog.py` alongside run-tonight.sh in the application root, with
  run-tonight.sh at the application root. Launch from
  that root. Keep the runner's sandbox checkout; worktrees are optional, not required.
  The runner and agent must see the same backlog. Provision skills/dependencies
  before starting; agents do not relocate the project or bootstrap missing skills.
- Only explicitly assigned items are eligible. If this mode requires active items
  and none exist, report the mismatch; this restriction does not apply interactively.
- The helper selects dependency-eligible `[ ]` items in file order. Overnight still
  checks whether prerequisite code has been integrated before dependent work. Run
  one runner per checkout; no automatic locking is implied. The runner resets
  unfinished `[~]` entries to `[ ]`; a timeout/reset does not undo code or attempt
  history. Three consecutive sessions with no terminal outcome stop the run for
  review; the runner requires an existing repo and initial commit. Coordinator owns
  outcomes and never resets another run's work.
  Agents inspect Git history and existing progress notes to decide what remains
  after an interrupted session.
- The runner does not require a clean checkout; inspect and preserve existing
  changes instead of committing broken work to satisfy preflight. Sprint runs
  still start on the declared sprint branch aligned with its remote-tracking tip.
  Do not switch away from uncommitted task-branch work to start a new run.

### Backlog creation and readiness

Use BACKLOG.md.example for new backlogs. Preserve `## [state] ID — Title` headings,
permanent unique IDs, explicit dependencies, scope, acceptance criteria, relevant
verification, and documentation impact. The owner and interactive `tech-lead` plan
future work together; architect can propose slices and resolve design decisions
but does not own backlog state or overnight delivery outcomes.

Generated entries start `[-]`. Promote to `[ ]` only when scope/contracts are
settled and dependencies/capabilities are understood. Separate a bounded discovery
task from implementation that depends on its answer. Priority order is not a
substitute for dependencies. Do not activate work merely because it was generated.

Runner claims its selected ready IDs as `[~]`; `overnight` is runner-only. Never
claim all pending work without an assignment or reset another run's active work.
Keep attempts and resume evidence
when requeueing an incomplete item. A dependency completed on another branch must
be present in this execution baseline before its consumers proceed.

